Inside Oracle EBS R12.2: What Do APPL_TOP, ORACLE_HOME, COMMON_TOP, and INST_TOP Really Do?

-in Oracle EBS R12.2 there are 3 Layers for File System:
 → Code (Applications Code)
 → Technology Stack
 → Instance-specific config

==============================================================
User Request
   ↓
APPL_TOP (Application Code) '/u01/.../fs1/EBSapps/appl'
   ↓
COMMON_TOP (Shared Resources) '/u01/.../fs1/EBSapps/comn'
   ↓
ORACLE_HOME (Execution Engine) '/u01/.../fs1/FMW_Home'
   ↓
INST_TOP (Configs + Logs '/u01/.../inst/apps/<SID>_<hostname>' 
===============================================================

- APPL_TOP contains the core application code for all EBS modules such as GL, PO, and INV.

- ORACLE_HOME includes the technology stack binaries like Forms, Reports, and WebLogic components.

- COMMON_TOP provides shared resources and utilities used across all application modules.

- INST_TOP holds instance-specific configurations, logs, and administrative scripts.

- In Oracle EBS R12.2, APPL_TOP and ORACLE_HOME exist in dual file systems (fs1 and fs2) to support online patching.

- INST_TOP remains single and is not duplicated across file systems. 
- changes per environment (Dev, Test, and Production)

- These components work together to process user requests from the UI down to execution and logging.
